WEBVTT

00:00.600 --> 00:05.250
A long running fight between two of 3D printing's best known names has reached its

00:05.250 --> 00:10.013
first verdict. A United States jury has awarded Stratasys approximately twenty seven

00:10.013 --> 00:14.606
point six million dollars in past damages, after finding that Bambu Lab infringed

00:14.606 --> 00:19.369
four of its 3D printing patents. Here is what the decision says, and why the dispute

00:19.369 --> 00:20.333
is far from over.

00:21.233 --> 00:25.087
The headline number is roughly twenty seven point six million dollars.

00:25.087 --> 00:28.115
Importantly, that figure covers past infringement only.

00:28.115 --> 00:32.575
Stratasys has not disclosed how the money is divided between the four patents, or

00:32.575 --> 00:35.658
between the individual Bambu entities named in the case.

00:35.658 --> 00:38.961
It is a jury award, not yet a final judgment from the court.

00:39.861 --> 00:44.275
The verdict followed a week long trial in the United States District Court for the

00:44.275 --> 00:46.697
Eastern District of Texas, Marshall Division.

00:46.697 --> 00:51.110
According to a statement issued by Stratasys, the jury found that all four patents

00:51.110 --> 00:55.254
presented at trial were both valid and infringed by the Bambu Lab defendants.

00:55.254 --> 00:59.721
That combination, validity plus infringement, is the strongest possible outcome for

00:59.721 --> 01:01.067
a patent holder at trial.

01:01.967 --> 01:06.365
Four corporate entities sat on the defence side: Shanghai Lunkuo Technology, Tuozhu

01:06.365 --> 01:10.021
Technology Limited, Shenzhen Tuozhu Technology, and Bambulab Limited.

01:10.021 --> 01:13.359
In the proceedings they were referred to collectively as Bambu.

01:13.359 --> 01:17.492
The verdict applies to that group, though the breakdown of damages between the

01:17.492 --> 01:19.929
individual companies has not been made public.

01:20.829 --> 01:24.482
Stratasys framed the result as a defence of its research investment.

01:24.482 --> 01:28.510
In a statement following the verdict the company said it remains focused on

01:28.510 --> 01:32.377
delivering cutting edge technologies to customers, and on protecting the

01:32.377 --> 01:35.170
intellectual property that underpins its innovation.

01:35.170 --> 01:39.252
Notably, the statement did not say whether Stratasys will seek an injunction

01:39.252 --> 01:42.099
restricting sales of the affected Bambu Lab products.

01:42.999 --> 01:47.430
Bambu Lab plans to appeal. The company told 3D Printing Industry that it appreciates

01:47.430 --> 01:51.861
the time and effort of the jury, and that while it respects the judicial process, it

01:51.861 --> 01:54.393
disagrees with the verdict reached in this case.

01:54.393 --> 01:58.824
It added that the law gives Bambu the right to seek post trial review and to appeal,

01:58.824 --> 02:01.409
and that it intends to use those legal processes.

02:02.309 --> 02:06.662
The decision is a significant victory for Stratasys, but it does not conclude the

02:06.662 --> 02:10.800
broader dispute. This was only the first of two patent infringement cases the

02:10.800 --> 02:15.045
company filed against Bambu Lab. The jury verdict may be followed by post trial

02:15.045 --> 02:19.129
motions and an appeal, and a final judgment will determine the formal relief

02:19.129 --> 02:20.741
actually awarded by the court.

02:21.641 --> 02:25.990
Stratasys filed its two lawsuits against Bambu Lab in August twenty twenty four.

02:25.990 --> 02:30.447
The original complaints alleged infringement of ten United States patents covering

02:30.447 --> 02:33.708
technologies used in desktop material extrusion 3D printers.

02:33.708 --> 02:38.165
Of those ten, just four were put in front of this jury, which gives a sense of how

02:38.165 --> 02:40.393
much of the dispute is still outstanding.

02:41.293 --> 02:45.932
The four patents considered by the jury cover purge towers, extrusion based layer

02:45.932 --> 02:50.685
deposition, and force detection systems. They are US patent nine four two one seven

02:50.685 --> 02:55.324
one three, on printing three dimensional parts with purge towers; seven five five

02:55.324 --> 03:00.077
five three five seven, on building objects with extrusion based layered deposition;

03:00.077 --> 03:04.716
and nine one six eight six nine eight together with ten five five six three eight

03:04.716 --> 03:08.381
one, both titled three dimensional printer with force detection.

03:09.281 --> 03:13.987
In plain terms, the purge tower patent broadly concerns printing material into a

03:13.987 --> 03:18.105
separate tower during changes between print heads or deposition lines.

03:18.105 --> 03:22.753
The three five seven patent concerns generating extrusion paths that fill small

03:22.753 --> 03:27.577
voids within printed layers. The six nine eight and three eight one patents belong

03:27.577 --> 03:31.989
to the same family, and concern force sensing at the extruder or tool head,

03:31.989 --> 03:36.755
including detecting contact with the build platform and identifying deflection or

03:36.755 --> 03:37.755
operating errors.

03:38.655 --> 03:41.878
Bambu Lab also pointed to related proceedings elsewhere.

03:41.878 --> 03:46.483
On the twenty fourth of April twenty twenty six, the Hague Local Division of the

03:46.483 --> 03:51.030
Unified Patent Court dismissed a Stratasys application for preliminary measures

03:51.030 --> 03:55.866
against Bambu Lab's H2C 3D printer. That case concerned European patent E P two nine

03:55.866 --> 04:00.413
six four four five zero, which belongs to the same family as the American purge

04:00.413 --> 04:01.161
tower patent.

04:02.061 --> 04:06.623
According to Bambu, the court concluded at the preliminary stage that the H2C did

04:06.623 --> 04:11.073
not fall within the scope of the European patent, and Stratasys did not appeal.

04:11.073 --> 04:15.466
A separate action brought by Bambu to invalidate that European patent is still

04:15.466 --> 04:20.084
pending. But the European ruling does not directly determine the American outcome:

04:20.084 --> 04:24.759
the proceedings involved different claims, different accused products and different

04:24.759 --> 04:29.490
legal standards, and it concerned preliminary measures rather than a final decision.

04:30.390 --> 04:34.922
Bambu also highlighted three inter partes review proceedings before the United

04:34.922 --> 04:39.629
States Patent Trial and Appeal Board. The Board found challenged claims of patent

04:39.629 --> 04:43.987
nine five nine two six six zero, covering heated build platform technology,

04:43.987 --> 04:47.125
unpatentable, and Bambu says Stratasys did not appeal.

04:47.125 --> 04:51.715
It also found challenged claims of patent eight five six two three two four, on

04:51.715 --> 04:56.422
networked 3D printing, unpatentable. Bambu says a request for Director Review was

04:56.422 --> 04:59.269
rejected, though the appeal period remained open.

05:00.169 --> 05:04.185
Context matters here. Neither of the patents the board struck down was among the

05:04.185 --> 05:07.999
four considered in this jury verdict. Patent nine five nine two six six zero

05:07.999 --> 05:12.165
appeared in the original complaint but never reached the jury, while eight five six

05:12.165 --> 05:15.076
two three two four forms part of the other Stratasys case.

05:15.076 --> 05:19.242
On the purge tower patent, the Board reached the opposite conclusion, finding Bambu

05:19.242 --> 05:23.106
had not shown the claims unpatentable. Bambu has appealed that to the Federal

05:23.106 --> 05:23.508
Circuit.

05:24.408 --> 05:28.533
So, where does that leave things? A Texas jury has found four Stratasys patents

05:28.533 --> 05:32.867
valid and infringed, and awarded roughly twenty seven point six million dollars for

05:32.867 --> 05:36.731
past infringement. Bambu Lab intends to seek post trial review and appeal.

05:36.731 --> 05:41.065
A final judgment is still to come, the question of an injunction is unanswered, and

05:41.065 --> 05:45.138
a second lawsuit plus parallel European and patent board proceedings are still

05:45.138 --> 05:48.323
running. Reporting by Michael Petch for 3D Printing Industry.
